Every custom constraint annotation must declare three attributes that the JSR-303 specification requires: `message()`, `groups()`, and `payload()`. Omit any of them and the validation framework throws an error at startup. These aren't optional — they're part of the contract that makes your annotation compatible with the standard validation API.

```java
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Documented
@Constraint(validatedBy = PhoneNumberValidator.class)
public @interface ValidPhoneNumber {
    String message() default "Invalid phone number format";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
    String region() default "US";
}
```

`@Constraint(validatedBy = ...)` is the link between annotation and validator — this is how the validation framework knows which class to instantiate. `@Retention(RUNTIME)` is critical: without it, the annotation is stripped at compile time and invisible to the validation framework at runtime. The `region()` attribute is custom — you can add any attributes your validation logic needs, and access them in the validator's `initialize()` method.

The validator class implements `ConstraintValidator<AnnotationType, ValueType>` and provides the actual validation logic in `isValid()`. There's an important convention: **always return `true` for null values**. Null checking is `@NotNull`'s job. If your validator rejects null, you force developers to always pair your annotation with `@NotNull`, and the error messages become confusing when both fire simultaneously.

```java
public class PhoneNumberValidator
        implements ConstraintValidator<ValidPhoneNumber, String> {
    private String region;

    @Override
    public void initialize(ValidPhoneNumber annotation) {
        this.region = annotation.region();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isValid(String value, ConstraintValidatorContext ctx) {
        if (value == null) return true;  // Let @NotNull handle nulls
        return value.matches("^\\+[1-9]\\d{1,14}$");
    }
}
```

`initialize()` runs once when the validator is created, giving you access to annotation attributes. `isValid()` runs on every validation call. The `ConstraintValidatorContext` parameter lets you customize the error message dynamically — useful when you want to include the invalid value or a specific reason in the message.

Spring manages validator instances as beans, which means you can **inject Spring dependencies into validators**. Need to check if an email is unique? Inject `UserRepository` with `@Autowired`. But be cautious: validators run on every request, so a database call in a validator adds latency to every API call. Validate format in the validator, check uniqueness in the service layer.

Field-level annotations validate one field in isolation. But some rules span multiple fields: "confirm password must match password," "end date must be after start date," "discount percentage can't exceed the tier limit." For these, you need a **class-level constraint** — an annotation placed on the class itself, with a validator that receives the entire object.

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = PasswordMatchValidator.class)
public @interface PasswordsMatch {
    String message() default "Passwords do not match";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}

public class PasswordMatchValidator
        implements ConstraintValidator<PasswordsMatch, PasswordChangeRequest> {
    @Override
    public boolean isValid(PasswordChangeRequest req, ConstraintValidatorContext ctx) {
        if (req.getNewPassword() == null) return true;
        return req.getNewPassword().equals(req.getConfirmPassword());
    }
}
```

Apply the annotation to the DTO class, not to individual fields. The validator receives the full object instance, so it can compare any combination of fields. The validation error appears at the class level in the `BindingResult`, not attached to a specific field — if you need the error associated with a particular field for frontend display, use `ctx.disableDefaultConstraintViolation()` and `ctx.buildConstraintViolationWithTemplate(msg).addPropertyNode("confirmPassword").addConstraintViolation()` to redirect the error to the right field.

The `get(String name, ObjectFactory<?> objectFactory)` method is the heart of a custom scope. Spring calls it every time a bean with your scope is needed — and your implementation decides whether to return an existing instance or create a new one via `objectFactory.getObject()`.

The `name` parameter is the bean name (not the scope name). The `ObjectFactory` is a callback that creates the bean using Spring's standard lifecycle — constructor, dependency injection, `@PostConstruct`, everything. You call it when no instance exists for the current context; you skip it and return the cached instance when one already exists.

```java
@Override
public Object get(String name, ObjectFactory<?> objectFactory) {
    String tenantId = TenantContext.getCurrentTenantId();
    Map<String, Object> beans = scopedBeans
        .computeIfAbsent(tenantId, k -> new ConcurrentHashMap<>());
    return beans.computeIfAbsent(name, k -> objectFactory.getObject());
}
```

The storage mechanism defines your scope's behavior. A `ConcurrentHashMap` keyed by tenant ID creates one bean instance per tenant — all requests from the same tenant share the same instance. The thread-safety of `ConcurrentHashMap` is essential here because multiple threads may handle requests for the same tenant simultaneously, and `computeIfAbsent` ensures only one instance is created even under race conditions.

Spring expects scopes to call destruction callbacks when a bean's context ends. Without this, `@PreDestroy` methods never fire, resources leak, and connection pools are never closed. The `registerDestructionCallback(String name, Runnable callback)` method stores these callbacks, and your code must invoke them at the right moment.

```java
@Override
public void registerDestructionCallback(String name, Runnable callback) {
    String tenantId = TenantContext.getCurrentTenantId();
    destructionCallbacks
        .computeIfAbsent(tenantId, k -> new ConcurrentHashMap<>())
        .put(name, callback);
}

public void endTenantSession(String tenantId) {
    Map<String, Runnable> callbacks = destructionCallbacks.remove(tenantId);
    if (callbacks != null) {
        callbacks.values().forEach(Runnable::run);
    }
    scopedBeans.remove(tenantId);
}
```

The `endTenantSession()` method isn't part of the `Scope` interface — it's your application's responsibility to call it when the tenant context ends. For a request scope, the servlet container signals when the request is complete. For your custom scope, **you** must define and trigger that signal. This is the hardest part of custom scopes: knowing when the context ends. In a multi-tenant app, it might be when the tenant's last active session expires. In a batch scope, it's when the job execution finishes.

Register your custom scope via `CustomScopeConfigurer`, a static `@Bean` method that runs early in the context lifecycle — before any scoped beans need to be resolved.

```java
@Configuration
public class TenantScopeConfig {
    @Bean
    public static CustomScopeConfigurer tenantScopeConfigurer() {
        CustomScopeConfigurer configurer = new CustomScopeConfigurer();
        configurer.addScope("tenant", new TenantScope());
        return configurer;
    }
}
```

The `static` keyword matters. `CustomScopeConfigurer` is a `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` — it modifies the bean factory before beans are created. Non-static `@Bean` methods in `@Configuration` classes require the configuration class to be instantiated first, which can cause ordering issues with scope registration.

When injecting a tenant-scoped bean into a singleton, you need a **scoped proxy** — just like injecting a request-scoped bean into a singleton. Without the proxy, the singleton gets one tenant-scoped instance at startup and uses it forever, regardless of which tenant is making the request.

```java
@Component
@Scope(value = "tenant", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class TenantConfig {
    private String theme;
    private List<String> enabledFeatures;
}
```

The proxy delegates to your scope's `get()` method on every method call, ensuring the correct tenant's instance is used. Before building a custom scope, verify that a simpler approach — like a `Map<TenantId, Config>` in a singleton — wouldn't achieve the same goal with less infrastructure.

The auto-configuration class looks like a regular `@Configuration` class with one critical addition: every bean is guarded by conditions. Class-level conditions gate the entire configuration; method-level conditions control individual beans.

```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(SmsClient.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(SmsProperties.class)
public class SmsAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public SmsClient smsClient(SmsProperties props) {
        return SmsClient.builder()
            .apiKey(props.getApiKey())
            .region(props.getRegion())
            .build();
    }

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public SmsService smsService(SmsClient client) {
        return new DefaultSmsService(client);
    }
}
```

`@ConditionalOnClass(SmsClient.class)` means this entire configuration is skipped if the SMS library isn't on the classpath. `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` on each bean means users can override any individual component by declaring their own. The `@AutoConfiguration` annotation (Spring Boot 2.7+) replaces the older `@Configuration` and signals that this class should be processed after user-defined configurations — ensuring user beans are registered first so `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` works correctly.

A good auto-configuration exposes its behavior through properties — not hardcoded values. `@ConfigurationProperties` creates a dedicated namespace (like `sms.*`) that users configure in their `application.properties`.

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "sms")
public class SmsProperties {
    private String apiKey;
    private String region = "us-east-1";
    private int maxRetries = 3;
    private Duration timeout = Duration.ofSeconds(5);
}
```

The auto-configuration class uses `@EnableConfigurationProperties(SmsProperties.class)` to register this class. Users configure it naturally: `sms.api-key=abc123`, `sms.region=eu-west-1`. Spring Boot's relaxed binding handles the kebab-case to camelCase conversion automatically.

The production footgun is **not providing defaults for optional properties**. If `sms.region` has no default and the user doesn't set it, the field is `null`, and your auto-configured bean might throw a NullPointerException deep inside a service call. Always provide sensible defaults in the properties class, and use `@Validated` with `@NotBlank` on properties that truly are required — this surfaces missing values at startup, not at runtime.

Auto-configuration is appropriate for shared libraries and internal platform concerns. For application-level bean setup, a regular `@Configuration` class is simpler and more explicit — don't over-engineer a feature that only one application uses.

Internal platform teams maintain starters for shared infrastructure: authentication, observability, feature flags, multi-tenancy. A well-built internal starter lets application teams add one dependency and get best-practice configuration automatically. Here's what separates a good internal starter from one that gets abandoned.

**Document what the starter configures.** A starter with no README gets reimplemented by the second team that needs it, because they can't tell what it does. List every bean it creates, every property it exposes, and every condition it evaluates.

**Always use `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`.** If your authentication starter doesn't allow overrides, the first team with a non-standard authentication flow will fork your starter instead of extending it. Every auto-configured bean should be replaceable.

**Version your properties namespace.** Once teams depend on `sms.api-key`, renaming it to `sms.credentials.api-key` is a breaking change. Treat property names as a public API — deprecate before removing.

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnMissingBean
public SmsClient smsClient(SmsProperties props) {
    return SmsClient.builder()
        .apiKey(props.getApiKey())
        .region(props.getRegion())
        .timeout(props.getTimeout())
        .build();
}
```

The `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` annotation on this bean is the escape hatch. Any application can declare its own `SmsClient` bean, and the starter's default silently backs off. Without it, the starter becomes a constraint rather than a convenience — and constrained teams stop using shared infrastructure.

The resolver needs a way to identify which parameters it should handle. A custom annotation is the cleanest approach — it's explicit, self-documenting, and doesn't interfere with other resolvers.

```java
@Target(ElementType.PARAMETER)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface CurrentUser { }
```

Keep the annotation on `PARAMETER` target only — it doesn't make sense on fields or methods. `RUNTIME` retention is required because Spring inspects annotations via reflection at runtime. The annotation is intentionally empty — its mere presence on a parameter is the signal. If you need to pass options (like "load full profile vs. lightweight principal"), add attributes to the annotation.

You could also resolve by parameter type alone — "any parameter of type `User` gets the current user" — but this is fragile. A `User` parameter might appear in non-authenticated endpoints, and the resolver would fail unexpectedly. The annotation makes the contract explicit: this parameter wants the authenticated user, and it's an error if no user is authenticated.

The resolver implements `HandlerMethodArgumentResolver` and provides the logic for both identifying supported parameters and resolving their values. The critical production detail is handling the case where the user isn't authenticated — your resolver should throw a clear exception, not return null.

```java
@Component
public class CurrentUserArgumentResolver implements HandlerMethodArgumentResolver {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public CurrentUserArgumentResolver(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean supportsParameter(MethodParameter parameter) {
        return parameter.hasParameterAnnotation(CurrentUser.class)
            && User.class.isAssignableFrom(parameter.getParameterType());
    }

    @Override
    public Object resolveArgument(MethodParameter parameter,
            ModelAndViewContainer mavContainer,
            NativeWebRequest webRequest,
            WebDataBinderFactory binderFactory) {
        Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
        if (auth == null || !auth.isAuthenticated()) {
            throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
        }
        String email = auth.getName();
        return userRepository.findByEmail(email)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED));
    }
}
```

The `supportsParameter()` check is dual: the annotation must be present **and** the parameter type must be assignable to `User`. This prevents the resolver from activating on a `@CurrentUser String name` parameter, which would cause a confusing ClassCastException. Register the resolver in a `WebMvcConfigurer` implementation to make Spring MVC aware of it.

The resolver's value becomes clear when you compare controller methods before and after. Without a resolver, every secured endpoint repeats the same extraction boilerplate, and the business logic is buried under infrastructure code.

**Before (boilerplate in every controller):**
```java
@GetMapping("/api/profile")
public ProfileResponse getProfile() {
    Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
    if (auth == null) throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
    User user = userRepository.findByEmail(auth.getName())
        .orElseThrow(() -> new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED));
    return profileService.buildProfile(user);
}
```

**After (resolver handles extraction):**
```java
@GetMapping("/api/profile")
public ProfileResponse getProfile(@CurrentUser User user) {
    return profileService.buildProfile(user);
}
```

The controller method now declares what it needs — an authenticated user — and the resolver provides it. If authentication logic changes (switching from email-based to ID-based lookup, or adding role pre-loading), you update one resolver, not fifty controller methods.

Register the resolver in `WebMvcConfigurer.addArgumentResolvers()`. The registration order matters if multiple resolvers might match the same parameter — Spring uses the first resolver that returns `true` from `supportsParameter()`. Custom resolvers are checked before built-in ones, so your resolver takes priority over Spring's default handling.

Every filter follows the same structure: do something before the request reaches the servlet, call the chain, then do something with the response. Wrapping the chain call in a `try/finally` ensures post-processing runs even when the controller throws an exception.

```java
@Component
@Order(1)
public class RequestLoggingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
        response.setHeader("X-Correlation-Id", correlationId);
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            long elapsed = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            log.info("[{}] {} {} completed in {}ms",
                correlationId, request.getMethod(), request.getRequestURI(), elapsed);
            MDC.remove("correlationId");
        }
    }
}
```

`OncePerRequestFilter` guarantees `doFilterInternal()` runs exactly once per request, even if the servlet container dispatches the request multiple times (forwards, includes, error dispatch). The `MDC.remove()` in `finally` prevents the correlation ID from leaking to the next request on the same thread — a subtle but critical bug in thread-pooled servers.

For URL-specific filtering or explicit ordering, use `FilterRegistrationBean` instead of `@Component`. This gives you `addUrlPatterns("/api/*")` for selective application and `setOrder(n)` for precise positioning in the filter chain. Lower order numbers execute first.

Spring Security has its own filter chain (`SecurityFilterChain`) that runs **inside** Tomcat's servlet filter chain. Your `@Component` filters run outside Spring Security — they see the request before authentication happens and after the response is committed.

This distinction matters when you're deciding where to put logic. Authentication and authorization belong in Spring Security's filter chain — use `SecurityFilterChain` configuration, not custom servlet filters. Cross-cutting concerns that are independent of security — logging, correlation IDs, rate limiting, response compression — belong in servlet filters.

A common production mistake is building a custom authentication filter as a plain servlet filter instead of integrating with Spring Security. The filter "works" in isolation, but it doesn't populate the `SecurityContext`, which means `@PreAuthorize`, `@Secured`, and Spring Security's authorization infrastructure don't know the user is authenticated. If you're doing authentication, integrate with Spring Security's `AuthenticationManager` and register your filter in the `SecurityFilterChain` — not as a standalone servlet filter.

```java
@Bean
public FilterRegistrationBean<RateLimitFilter> rateLimitFilter() {
    FilterRegistrationBean<RateLimitFilter> reg = new FilterRegistrationBean<>();
    reg.setFilter(new RateLimitFilter(100));
    reg.addUrlPatterns("/api/*");
    reg.setOrder(2);
    return reg;
}
```

This rate limit filter applies only to `/api/*` URLs and runs after the logging filter (`@Order(1)`). Business logic filters like this belong in the servlet layer — they're independent of Spring Security and should execute regardless of authentication status.

The power of interceptors is best demonstrated by annotation-driven logic — something that's impossible in a filter because filters have no access to handler metadata.

This interceptor enforces API versioning by reading a custom `@ApiVersion` annotation from the controller method and comparing it against a request header.

```java
@Component
public class ApiVersionInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {

    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response, Object handler) throws Exception {
        if (handler instanceof HandlerMethod hm) {
            ApiVersion version = hm.getMethodAnnotation(ApiVersion.class);
            if (version != null) {
                String requested = request.getHeader("X-Api-Version");
                if (!version.value().equals(requested)) {
                    response.sendError(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value(),
                        "Expected API version " + version.value());
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

The `handler` parameter is cast to `HandlerMethod`, which gives access to the controller method's annotations, parameter types, and return type. Returning `false` from `preHandle()` aborts the request — the controller method is never invoked.

Register the interceptor in `WebMvcConfigurer.addInterceptors()` with path patterns to control which URLs it applies to. Unlike filters, interceptors support both inclusion and exclusion patterns, making it easy to apply logic to `/api/**` while excluding `/api/public/**`.

The `afterCompletion()` hook deserves mention: it runs after the response is fully rendered, even if an exception occurred. Use it for cleanup — clearing `ThreadLocal` values, releasing resources — where `finally` blocks in a filter would serve the same purpose but with less clarity about the execution phase.

A well-implemented converter handles case-insensitivity and produces clear error messages when the input is invalid. This matters because path variables and query parameters come from users — and users send unexpected values.

```java
@Component
public class StringToOrderStatusConverter implements Converter<String, Order.Status> {
    @Override
    public Order.Status convert(String source) {
        try {
            return Order.Status.valueOf(source.toUpperCase());
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Invalid order status: '" + source + "'. Valid values: "
                + Arrays.toString(Order.Status.values()));
        }
    }
}
```

Register the converter in `WebMvcConfigurer`, and every `@PathVariable` and `@RequestParam` of type `Order.Status` is converted automatically.

```java
@RestController
public class OrderController {
    @GetMapping("/api/orders")
    public List<Order> getByStatus(@RequestParam Order.Status status) {
        return orderService.findByStatus(status);
    }
}
```

Spring calls your converter transparently. A request to `/api/orders?status=pending` converts "pending" to `Order.Status.PENDING` before the controller method is invoked. An invalid value like `/api/orders?status=invalid` triggers the `IllegalArgumentException` from your converter, which Spring wraps in a 400 Bad Request response.

For locale-aware formatting — parsing `$1,234.56` in US format vs `1.234,56` in German — implement `Formatter<T>` instead of `Converter<S, T>`. Formatters add a `Locale` parameter to both `parse()` and `print()`, making them the right choice for currency, dates, and numbers that vary by locale. Both are registered through the same `addFormatters()` method in `WebMvcConfigurer`.

Spring routes exceptions to the **most specific** matching handler. This means you can build a handler hierarchy: specific business exceptions at the top, generic catch-alls at the bottom.

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(EntityNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(EntityNotFoundException ex) {
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
            HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
        problem.setTitle("Resource Not Found");
        return problem;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleGeneral(Exception ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("Unhandled exception at {}: {}", request.getRequestURI(),
            ex.getMessage(), ex);
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatus(
            HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
        problem.setTitle("Internal Server Error");
        problem.setDetail("An unexpected error occurred");
        return problem;
    }
}
```

The `ProblemDetail` class (Spring 6 / Boot 3) implements RFC 7807 — a standard format for HTTP error responses. Using it means your errors follow an industry convention that client libraries and API gateways can parse automatically. The generic `Exception` handler at the bottom catches anything not handled by a more specific method — it logs the full exception internally and returns a sanitized message to the client.

Extending `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` gives you pre-built handling for Spring MVC's built-in exceptions — `MethodArgumentNotValidException` for validation failures, `HttpMessageNotReadableException` for malformed JSON, `NoHandlerFoundException` for 404s. Override specific methods to customize the response format while keeping the rest of Spring's default handling.

A good health indicator wraps everything in a try-catch, includes diagnostic details in the response, and completes quickly. Health checks that take longer than 2-3 seconds delay Kubernetes readiness determination and can cascade into pod scheduling problems.

```java
@Component
public class PaymentGatewayHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {
    private final PaymentGatewayClient client;

    public PaymentGatewayHealthIndicator(PaymentGatewayClient client) {
        this.client = client;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            PingResponse response = client.ping();
            long latency = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            if (response.isSuccess()) {
                return Health.up()
                    .withDetail("latency_ms", latency)
                    .withDetail("version", response.getApiVersion())
                    .build();
            }
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("reason", response.getErrorMessage())
                .build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("Payment gateway health check failed", e);
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("error", e.getMessage())
                .withDetail("type", e.getClass().getSimpleName())
                .build();
        }
    }
}
```

Every failure path returns `Health.down()` instead of propagating the exception. The `withDetail()` calls attach diagnostic information that appears in the JSON response — useful for dashboards and incident response.

For non-critical dependencies that shouldn't take the pod out of rotation when they fail, use **health groups**. Configure `management.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include=db,diskSpace` to exclude your payment gateway check from the readiness probe. The payment gateway health still appears in the full health response for monitoring, but its status doesn't affect Kubernetes scheduling decisions. This distinction between "the app is healthy enough to receive traffic" and "every dependency is fully operational" prevents cascading failures where a flaky external service triggers unnecessary pod restarts across your cluster.
